WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
This is the brief on how the Great Depression and FDR transformed Georgia.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
It's kind of crazy how a tiny beetle and a future president's search for physical healing

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
teamed up to bring electricity and economic survival to the rural South.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
First, even before the 1929 stock market crash, Georgia was already in a massive depression

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.000
thanks to a tiny insect, the boll weevil. It wiped out the state's main cash crop,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
cotton, forcing farmers to just abandon their land.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Think of it like a single computer glitch totally wiping out a video game's entire economy.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Because Georgia relied on one crop, this tiny bug collapsed everything.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Second, into this devastated landscape stepped Franklin D. Roosevelt,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
visiting Warm Springs to treat his polio. While there, he got a shock.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
His rural electric bill was four times higher than his New York bill,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
because the area lacked basic power lines. I mean, how rare is it for a president's

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
personal annoyance over a utility bill to actually change history?

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
That specific frustration inspired his New Deal programs,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
like the Rural Electrification Administration, to bring power to poor farmers.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Finally, you'd think everyone would want this help, but Georgia Governor Eugene Talmadge

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
fiercely opposed the New Deal, claiming it gave the federal government too much power.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
It's pretty ironic that a governor tried to block free federal aid during a literal depression,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
even vetoing relief money. But desperate voters didn't buy it,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.000
overwhelmingly supporting FDR over Talmadge in 1936.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Ultimately, Georgia's survival through the Great Depression proves how local disasters

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
and a president's personal experiences can spark massive national progress.